/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain WATER

  /* Neutral island A. Default zone. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    land_percent 8
    base_size 6
    land_position 15 50
  }

  /* Neutral island B. Same default zone as A, so the zone model predicts a
     connection between exactly these two. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    land_percent 8
    base_size 6
    land_position 85 50
  }

  /* The player island. Its own zone, so the zone model predicts it connects to
     nothing at all. */
  create_player_lands
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    land_percent 8
    base_size 6
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  create_object FORAGE
  {
    number_of_objects 25
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

  create_connect_same_land_zones
  {
    replace_terrain WATER ROAD
    terrain_cost WATER 8
    terrain_cost GRASS 1
    terrain_cost DIRT 1
    terrain_size ROAD 1 0
  }

/*
RMSTEST_48 - does the same-land-zones connection command really group by zone?
Feeds: preview-design Sec.6.5, connections.ts, Sec.15 item 21

QUESTION
The connection command named for same land zones shipped modelled as an exact
synonym for the connect-all-lands command - every land origin, every pair. A
review caught that the same session it landed, and the corrected model is: group
the lands by their zone, connect every pair WITHIN each zone, never across
zones. That is the shape the connect-team-lands command already has, one level
down.

The correction is right on its face - a synonym would not need its own command -
but it comes from two independent community references rather than from the
guide this section otherwise cites, and it has never been put to the engine.

WHAT MAKES THE TWO MODELS SEPARABLE
On an unmodified script, neutral lands all share one default zone and each
player land gets a zone of its own. So:

  same-zone model  - the two neutral lands connect to each other and NOTHING
                     connects to the player land. One path.
  all-lands model  - all three pairs connect. Three paths.

That is a factor of three in road tiles and a completely different picture, so
it does not need a subtle instrument.

METHOD
Deep water base with three separated islands, so a connection has to cross water
and is drawn as a visible road. The two neutral lands are pinned by position at
opposite ends of the middle row; the player land is not pinnable by attribute
and lands wherever the default ring puts it, which is far enough from both to be
told apart by geometry.

The water cost is set high and the road replacement explicit, so every path is a
road ribbon on water and the road tile count is close to proportional to the
number of connections made.

PREDICTIONS, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN
  One road, running roughly along the middle row between the two pinned islands,
  and no road touching the player island
      -> the corrected zone-grouping model is right. Strike the verify marker on
         Sec.6.5 and record it as engine-confirmed rather than community-sourced.

  Three roads, one of them reaching the player island
      -> the original reading was right after all, the command IS a synonym, and
         connections.ts needs its zone grouping reverted. Note this would also
         mean the corrected model was adopted on community sources against a
         plainer reading, which is worth writing down as a process result and
         not only as a code fix.

  No roads at all
      -> the map is wrong, not the model. Most likely the water cost made every
         path unaffordable. Raise the cost or shorten the gaps and rerun; do not
         read this as evidence for either model.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), ONE PLAYER. Three runs.

One player is deliberate. It gives exactly one player land, so the zone count is
two and the arithmetic above is as simple as it can be. More players multiply
the pairs under the all-lands model and make the two predictions differ by more,
but they also crowd the map and make the geometry harder to attribute.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches ROAD
  Count road patches and read their bounding boxes. The geometry is the answer,
  not the tile total - one long road and three short ones can total the same.

THREE RUNS because path length varies with where the ring puts the player land,
and because a single generation where the player island happens to sit next to a
neutral one is ambiguous under both models.
*/
